f the phrase gets a different label.\n\nI'm testing this against tricky cases like \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein,\" where I need to decide if \"syndrome\" counts as part of the disease name or as the head noun, which affects how the surrounding words are classified.\n\nI'm settling on a final formulation: a disease span is Modifier when it premodifies a non-disease head within the same noun phrase, but SpecificDisease when it serves as the nominal head itself, including as a prepositional complement or clausal subject/object—determined by each occurrence's own syntax rather than its surface string or labels elsewhere. I'm also adding negative constraints excluding coordinated/multi-disease mentions and generic disease-class terms, plus noting the premodifier branch doesn't apply when the following noun is itself disease-denoting rather than a non-disease head.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Disease Span Role Rule (Premodifier vs. Referential Head):**\n  - **IF** a disease-denoting span is an attributive premodifier, meaning it is immediately followed within the same noun phrase by a non-disease nominal head that it restricts (e.g., a population, case, risk, or gene/protein noun), **THEN** label the span **Modifier**.\n  - **IF** the disease-denoting span is itself the nominal head of its noun phrase, including when that noun phrase is the complement of a preposition such as \"of\" or \"with,\" or is a clausal subject or object that refers to the disease as an entity, **THEN** label the span **SpecificDisease**, even when a preceding frame noun (e.g., \"history,\" \"risk,\" \"patients\") could be read as taking the disease as a qualifier.\n  - Decide each occurrence by its own syntactic role. The surface string and any labels it received elsewhere in the document are not evidence.\n  - **Negative constraint:** Do not apply this rule to spans that form part of a coordinated or multi-disease mention (CompositeMention), or to generic category terms that denote a class of diseases (DiseaseClass). Resolve those by their own definitions. The premodifier branch also does not apply when the span is followed by another disease-denoting noun, because the span may then be part of a single disease name.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1699, "output_tokens": 3904, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 3468}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}